Core principle: Record events and executions, not just prompt/response pairs. An interruption is a state transition, not automatically a new prompt.
Data model
event_id — unique per event


execution_id — unique per AI run


parent_event_id — links related events (preserves lineage)


timestamp — exact chronology


event_type — prompt / interruption / partial_output / completion / cancellation


execution_status — started / in_progress / completed / interrupted / cancelled / failed


content — what was said/produced


completion_state — complete vs partial


Interruption classification (decide this first) When a user interrupts, classify what it is before recording it:
Stop/cancel — no new content


Clarification — doesn’t change the task


Correction/modification — changes the current task


New instruction — different task entirely


Unrelated request


Only categories 3–5 spawn a new execution. Categories 1–2 just change status on the existing one.
Flow patterns
Normal:
USER_PROMPT → EXECUTION_STARTED → RESPONSE_COMPLETED

Interrupted with stop only:
USER_PROMPT → EXECUTION_STARTED → PARTIAL_OUTPUT → INTERRUPTION → EXECUTION_INTERRUPTED

Interrupted with new/modified instruction:
USER_PROMPT → EXECUTION_STARTED → PARTIAL_OUTPUT → INTERRUPTION 
→ EXECUTION_INTERRUPTED → NEW_INSTRUCTION (parent=original) → NEW_EXECUTION → NEW_RESPONSE

Rules to enforce
Never overwrite or delete the original prompt/execution — append, don’t mutate.


Partial AI output is preserved with status partial/interrupted, not silently discarded or marked “completed.”


New instructions triggered by an interruption always link back (parent_event_id) to the execution they interrupted — this is how you keep lineage instead of a flat transcript.


If multiple interruptions stack up, each gets its own event so you can always answer “what belonged to which execution.”


That’s the whole thing: give every event an identity, give every execution a status, classify interruptions before recording them, and never rewrite history — only append to it.

